course: reflection
lesson: 22
title: Lonely and different: accents, big brothers and being too much
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Moving to the other side of the world can make your own voice feel like a target: Holly's family moved from England to Australia, kids copied her accent with "Cup of tea, guv'nor?", and at night she thought: "Everyone laughs when I talk. I don't belong here. I want to go home to Nan."
explain: The first month she cried every night and barely spoke in class, so nobody would hear the accent; by month three a girl at netball asked her round and she started to laugh again; after a year she had a bit of an Aussie twang herself.
explain: What she came to understand: moving countries is hard for anyone, homesickness is the ache for the place and people you left, and teasing about accents is often curiosity done badly; accents fade or become something people like.
explain: Video calls with Nan and that one friendly girl helped; refusing to talk did not, because silence kept her alone with the worst story in her head.
explain: Loneliness has many shapes: a big brother who shuts his door at 13, friends who say you're "too loud", being picked last, a far-away farm, a month off ill; the thread through all of them is that it is rarely about something wrong with you, and real friends like you as you are.
fact: Homesickness is the sad ache for the home and people you left; it is common after a move and usually fades over months.
fact: "Pom" is Australian slang, often teasing, for a British person; accent teasing is often curiosity done badly.
fact: Children who move often pick up some of the new accent within a year or so.
fact: Refusing to talk to hide something about yourself usually keeps you lonely for longer.
fact: Teenagers often want more time alone and with friends their own age; it does not mean they have stopped loving their younger brother or sister.
fact: Some people prefer quiet friends and some like loud ones; real friends like you as you are, and you don't have to switch yourself off.
fact: Being picked last again and again hurts; teachers can choose teams in fairer ways if they are told.
fact: A club that leaves people out on purpose hurts; making your own fun or your own club is often better than begging to join.
fact: Living far from friends, for example in the countryside, can be lonely; weekend clubs and planned visits help.
fact: After a long time away, friendships may have shifted; joining back in slowly and inviting people round helps more than being cross.
fact: An inside joke is one only a group understands; other shared interests can connect you to people too.
fact: People can have more than one best friend; a new friend does not cancel an old friendship.
fact: Sometimes we think we were left out on purpose when it was a mistake; asking is better than assuming the worst.
exercise: Kids in Australia copied Holly's British accent. What was the teasing most often about? | Curiosity done badly, not deep dislike | A plan to make her move back to England | Her accent being wrong and needing a fix | The other kids being jealous of her clothes
exercise: How did Holly's feelings change over a year? | From crying every night to laughing again | From happy to more miserable every single month | From scared to angry at everyone in Australia | They never changed and she stayed silent all year
exercise: What is homesickness? | A sad ache for the home and people you left | An illness you catch from the dust and damp in a new house | Feeling sick of being at home all day | A cold you get on a long plane journey
exercise: Why did refusing to talk not help Holly? | It kept her alone with her worries | It made her accent stronger and stronger | It made the other kids copy her more | It is against the rules in Australian schools
exercise: Noah's big brother turned 13 and shuts his door. What is the most likely reason? | Teenagers often want more time alone and with friends | His brother has stopped loving him completely and wants him gone | Noah did something terrible that he won't say | His brother is ill and needs to stay in bed
exercise: What helped Noah? | Finding a hobby of his own | Knocking on the door every five minutes | Annoying his brother on purpose until he came out | Telling his parents to take the door off
exercise: Ava's friends say she's "too loud" and stop inviting her. What is true? | Real friends like you as you are | She must go quiet forever to keep friends | Loud people never have any real friends | She should shout louder until they listen
exercise: Archie is picked last in PE every week. What helped? | Telling the PE teacher, who changed how teams were picked | Pretending he didn't care at all | Skipping PE by faking illness each week | Picking the very same kids last whenever he got to be captain
exercise: Sophie's friends make a club and won't let her join. What worked? | Starting her own club that other kids joined | Begging them every single day until they finally let her in | Telling the teacher to close their club | Pretending she never wanted to join anyway
exercise: Max lives far out in the countryside and weekends are long. What helped? | Joining a weekend sports club | Staying in his room all weekend | Moving to the city on his own | Waiting for friends to come to him
exercise: Lucy was ill for a month and her friends now have new friends. What helped? | Inviting her friends round and joining back in slowly | Being cross with them for forgetting her while she was off ill | Telling the new friends to go away | Staying home another month to make them sorry
exercise: Jacob doesn't play video games and misses the jokes. What helped? | Finding friends who like football like him | Pretending he knows all the games and laughing along at every joke | Begging for a games console every day | Laughing at jokes he doesn't understand
exercise: Isla's best friend has a new best friend. What is true? | People can have more than one best friend | A new best friend always replaces the old one | Isla should be mean to the new girl | Friends can only ever have one friend each
exercise: How did Isla's story end? | They talked and became a trio | She found a different best friend | They stopped talking forever | The new girl moved away
exercise: Tom's party invitation got lost and he thought, "She didn't want me there." What should he do? | Ask her | Never speak to her again | Tell everyone she left him out | Throw a bigger party the same day
test: A boy in Sydney calls Holly a "Pom" and grins. What's this most likely about? | Teasing curiosity about her British accent, not real hate | A serious threat she should be very afraid of | A sign she should never speak in class again | A secret code meaning she isn't allowed to join the netball team
test: Holly's new mate goes, "Reckon you'll sound like one of us by Christmas, sweet as." ("Sweet as" means great, fine, in Australia and NZ.) What does this show? | The accent is becoming something friendly, not a problem | The mate is making fun of her every single day | Holly must lose her accent to have friends | Australians never like British accents at all
test: Noah thinks: "We used to be best mates. Now I'm just the annoying little brother." What's the kinder, truer view? | His brother is growing up and still loves him | His brother hates him now and always will, whatever Noah does | Noah should never talk to him again | It's Noah's fault for not growing up faster
test: Ava's gran from Liverpool says, "Ah, there's nowt wrong with being loud, girl, that's just you." ("Nowt" means nothing.) What is the advice? | Find friends who like her energy | Go quiet so the old friends come back | Shout at them until they invite her | Never talk at school again
test: Archie says, "I don't care about being picked last, it's whatever." Is that true? | Probably not; pretending not to care often hides hurt | Yes; nobody minds being picked last | Yes; being picked last is an honour | No; he secretly wants to be picked first every time and be captain
test: What would happen if Sophie kept begging to join the club every day? | It would probably hurt more and not get her in | They would definitely let her in by Friday | The club would close and everyone would be friends | Her begging would make her the club's leader
test: Max's mum says, "We live in the middle of nowhere, love, not the moon." (The middle of nowhere means a place very far from towns.) What does she suggest? | Friends can still be made through clubs and visits | Max will never have any friends out there | They should move to the moon to be happier | Living in the country is the same as living in town
test: Lucy thinks, "I've been replaced. They didn't even miss me." What happened when she invited them round? | Things got better | Nobody came at all | They said she was replaced | They brought the new friends to argue
test: Jacob's classmate goes, "bro doesn't even know what a creeper is, he's so out of the loop." (A creeper is a monster in the game Minecraft.) What helped Jacob? | Finding other interests he shares with people | Pretending he plays Minecraft every night | Crying until his parents bought him a console | Telling the classmate games are for babies
test: Isla thinks, "she's replaced me." In a film, two best friends who get a third friend always fight. In real life? | Many become a happy trio after talking | Third friends always break up best friends | Talking about it always makes it worse | Old friends are always forgotten
test: Tom's sister says, "no cap, she probably just lost it, ask her." ("No cap" means honestly.) What actually happened when Tom asked? | It was a mistake and the invitation had got lost | She said she never wanted him there | She had invited everyone except boys | She laughed at him in front of everybody in the class
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises homesickness consequent upon relocation abroad? | It is a common sorrow for the former home which customarily diminishes over months | It is a contagious ailment acquired upon arrival in a foreign country | It is permanent and incapable of any amelioration | It is experienced exclusively by adults and never by children
exam: Identify the principal reason for which an elder sibling of thirteen years may cease to play with a younger sibling. | Adolescents commonly seek greater privacy and the company of their peers | The elder sibling has invariably ceased to hold the younger in affection | The younger sibling has committed a grave and undisclosed offence | Persons of thirteen are prohibited from playing games with children
exam: It is given that a pupil believes herself deliberately excluded from an occasion. Select the response consistent with the established facts. | Enquiry is preferable to assumption, as the exclusion may have been accidental | The worst interpretation is invariably the correct one | Enquiry invariably aggravates the situation and ought to be avoided | Exclusion from an occasion is in all cases deliberate
exam: In respect of the possession of more than one close friend, determine which statement is correct. | A person may maintain more than one close friendship concurrently | The acquisition of a new friend automatically terminates all prior friendships | Close friendships are limited by custom to one per person | A new friendship obliges the abandonment of the former friend
